course: world
lesson: 30
title: Balance in a food web
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: When an animal eats, most of the energy in its food is used up for moving, keeping warm and living, and only a small part (about a tenth) is built into its body and can pass on to whoever eats it.
explain: So each step of a food chain has much less energy than the one below: there must be far more grass than zebras, and far more zebras than lions, which is why big predators are always rare.
explain: In a food web everything is connected, so a change to one living thing spreads: when wolves disappeared from Yellowstone Park, deer grew so many that they ate the young trees by the rivers; when wolves came back, the trees grew again.
explain: Sea otters eat sea urchins, and sea urchins eat kelp seaweed; where otters were hunted away, the urchins multiplied and ate whole kelp forests, and all the fish living in the kelp lost their home.
explain: To guess what happens when one living thing disappears, follow the arrows: what it ate will grow in number, what ate it will go hungry and must switch food, shrink or move away.
fact: Only about a tenth of the energy at one step of a food chain passes on to the next step.
fact: Most of the energy an animal eats is used for moving, growing, keeping warm and staying alive.
fact: Because energy is lost at each step, there are always far fewer predators than plant-eaters.
fact: Food chains rarely have more than four or five steps, because too little energy is left at the top.
fact: If a predator disappears, its prey usually increases in number.
fact: If prey animals increase too much, they can eat too many plants and harm the whole habitat.
fact: If a prey animal disappears, the predators that ate it must find other food, move away or decline.
fact: An animal that eats many kinds of food survives the loss of one food more easily than an animal that eats only one.
fact: Predators help keep a habitat healthy by stopping plant-eaters from growing too many.
fact: In Yellowstone Park, returning wolves reduced the number of deer and the riverside trees grew back.
fact: Sea otters protect kelp forests by eating the sea urchins that eat kelp.
fact: A field of crops can feed many more people directly than through feeding animals for meat.
exercise: Why are there far fewer lions than zebras in the savanna? | Little of the zebras' energy reaches the lions | Lions have fewer cubs than zebras have foals each year | Zebras hide most of the lions' cubs | Lions prefer to live alone in deserts
exercise: About how much of the energy at one step of a food chain passes to the next step? | About a tenth | About half | Almost all of it | None of it
exercise: Where does most of the energy an animal eats go? | Into moving, keeping warm and living | Into fat it stores for ever | Back into the soil as fresh food | Into growing bigger every single day
exercise: Why do food chains rarely have more than five steps? | Too little energy is left at the top | Top predators always eat plants too | Big animals do not like long chains | Plants stop growing after five steps
exercise: What usually happens to rabbits if all the foxes are removed? | Their numbers grow | Their numbers fall at once | They start to eat each other | They stop having babies
exercise: What happened in Yellowstone when wolves were gone? | Deer grew in number and ate young trees | Deer became fewer and the young trees spread | Trees grew taller and thicker than ever before | Rivers dried up because the wolves had gone
exercise: Why did kelp forests vanish where sea otters were hunted? | Sea urchins multiplied and ate the kelp | Otters had been planting the kelp | Fish ate the kelp without the otters | The water got much too warm for the kelp to grow
exercise: What happens to a predator if its only prey disappears? | It must find other food, move or decline | It turns into a plant-eater overnight and eats grass | It always grows in number | Nothing at all changes for it
exercise: Which animal survives the loss of one food more easily? | One that eats many kinds of food | One that eats only one kind of food | One that is the biggest predator | One that lives in only one tree
exercise: How do predators help keep a habitat healthy? | They stop plant-eaters becoming too many | They make extra food for the plants | They eat all the decomposers | They keep all the plants from growing too tall
exercise: Why can a field of wheat feed more people as bread than as meat from cattle fed on it? | Energy is lost when cattle use the food | Cattle make the wheat grow slower | Meat has no energy left in it at all | Bread is heavier than the meat
exercise: Why is a big predator at the top of a food chain often the first to suffer when the habitat is damaged? | It is rare and depends on many steps below it | It is the smallest animal in the habitat, so it is weak | It eats only plants that die first | It has no enemies to protect it
exercise: If too many deer eat the young trees, what may happen to birds nesting in those trees? | They lose their homes and become fewer | They grow in number with more space | They begin to eat the deer instead | Nothing, birds do not need trees
exercise: To guess the effect of losing one animal from a food web, what should you look at? | What it ate and what ate it | Only how big and heavy it was | Only what colour its fur was | Only how fast it could run away
test: An island has grass, rabbits and foxes. A disease kills most of the rabbits. What happens to the grass and the foxes? | The grass grows more and the foxes go hungry | The grass dies and the foxes grow fat | Both the grass and the foxes grow more | Nothing happens to either of them
test: A meadow has 1000 kilograms of grass a year. Roughly how many kilograms of grasshoppers can it feed? | About 100 | About 1000 | About 500 | About 2000
test: A farmer kills all the owls on his farm because they scare his chickens. What is likely to happen next? | Mice increase and eat more of his grain | Mice decrease because owls protected them | The grain grows better without the owls | His chickens start to catch the mice
test: Why is it impossible to have a habitat with more lions than zebras for long? | The lions would not get enough food energy | Zebras would eat all the lions | Zebras would run away to other lands | Lions eat only grass in winter
test: A pond food web has algae, water fleas, small fish and a pike. Someone adds many more pike. What happens first? | Small fish become fewer | Algae become fewer | Water fleas become fewer | The pike all become thin at once
test: Bees pollinate a meadow's flowers, birds eat the seeds, and foxes hunt the birds. Bees vanish. Who may suffer, even though they never eat bees? | The birds and then the foxes | Only the flowers, nobody else | Nobody, because foxes eat birds | Only the bees' nests
test: A koala eats only eucalyptus leaves; a fox eats mice, berries and insects. Which is in more danger if one of its foods disappears? | The koala | The fox | Both the same | Neither of them
test: Why is a tiger much rarer than a deer in the same forest? | Each step up the food chain has much less energy | Tigers have far more enemies than deer | Tigers need much more water than deer | Deer have more stripes for hiding
test: A town removes all fallen leaves and dead logs from a park every week for years. What slowly happens? | The soil gets poorer for the plants | The plants grow faster and greener than ever | The predators get much more prey to eat | Nothing, because dead things are useless
test: Sea otters return to a coast. What happens to the kelp forest over the years? | It grows back as urchins become fewer | It disappears completely as the otters eat the kelp | It stays exactly the same as it was before | It is replaced by bare sand and stones
test: why r there way fewer foxes than rabbits | Only a little energy passes up each step of the chain | Foxes just have fewer babies than rabbits, by pure chance | Rabbits eat the foxes' food | Foxes live only in the winter
test: if the wolves all go wot happens to the deer | They usually increase and can eat too many plants | They usually decrease straight away | Nothing at all changes for them | They start eating other animals
test: my sister says predators are bad for a habitat and we should get rid of them. true? | No, they keep plant-eaters from growing too many | Yes, a habitat is healthier with no predators | Yes, without them all animals live in peace | No, but only because they eat the plants
test: Tom reckons almost all the energy an animal eats goes into the next animal. right? | No, only about a tenth passes on | Yes, nearly all of it passes right on | Yes, about half of it gets passed on | No, none of it ever passes on at all
exam: State the approximate proportion of energy transferred from one step of a food chain to the next. | One tenth | One half | Nine tenths | The whole
exam: Identify the principal reason for which predators are invariably fewer than plant-eaters. | Energy is lost at each step of the food chain | Predators reproduce more slowly from choice | Predators are hunted by plant-eaters | Plants are fewer than predators
exam: Whereas a predator disappears from a habitat, determine the usual consequence for its prey. | The prey usually increases in number | The prey usually declines in number | The prey usually departs the habitat | The prey usually remains unaltered
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the return of wolves to Yellowstone Park. | The deer were reduced and the riverside trees recovered | The deer increased and the trees perished along the riverbanks | The wolves consumed the trees | The rivers dried up entirely
